.Dd October 25, 2026
.Dt MOUNT 8
.Os Werewolf
.Sh NAME
.Nm mount
.Nd attach filesystems without relaxing mount restrictions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl t Ar type
.Op Fl o Ar options
.Ar source target
.Nm
.Fl Fl bind
.Ar source target
.Nm
.Fl o Ar remount Ns Op ,options
.Ar target
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The Werewolf
.Nm
utility mounts, binds and remounts the filesystems used during boot.
It is normally called by
.Xr init 8 .
It does not read fstab, probe filesystem types, resolve labels or
configure loop devices.
.Pp
Supported types are proc, sysfs, securityfs, cgroup2, devtmpfs, devpts,
tmpfs, ext4 and iso9660.
Targets are restricted to the boot filesystem locations beneath
.Pa /proc , /sys , /dev , /run , /tmp , /var/tmp , /data , /boot , /mnt
and
.Pa /oci .
Mounts over the root, /etc or /usr are refused.
Paths must be absolute, without dot components, and are resolved without symlinks.
.Pp
New mounts are nosuid and noexec, with nodev except for device filesystems
and nosymfollow except for proc, sysfs and devtmpfs.
The symfollow option withholds nosymfollow from a new mount or bind.
Binds retain the source's restrictions.
Remounts only add restrictions; rw and symfollow are refused on remount.
The suid, dev, exec and strictatime options are refused.
.Pp
Mounts are built and restricted while detached, then attached.
The utility keeps CAP_SYS_ADMIN alone and installs a system call filter
before performing mount operations.
After
.Xr fence 8 ,
ordinary processes cannot mount even through this utility.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Successful operations produce no output.
Failures include the kernel's reason when available.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fence 8 ,
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr mount-broker 8
